;; Wiggum spec generator
;;
;; Takes a description and produces a filled implementation spec in EDN
;; format, ready to run as its own Wiggum loop.
;;
;; Usage:
;;   1. Set :description below. One line for SIMPLE/MEDIUM, a paragraph or
;;      more for COMPLEX. Optionally append a provider keyword:
;;        :ollama  :kimi  :glm  :openrouter  :anthropic (default)
;;      Examples:
;;        'A CLI tool that watches a directory and prints new file summaries'
;;        'An HTMX todo app in Clojure with SQLite :ollama'
;;        'A Kafka consumer lag monitor dashboard :kimi'
;;        'A Clojure TUI options-trading research workbench backed by
;;         IBKR + DuckDB + Claude Code as agent (see options-trader.edn). :glm'
;;   2. Provider env vars (must be set if non-default):
;;        :kimi       MOONSHOT_API_KEY
;;        :glm        ZAI_API_KEY
;;        :openrouter OPENROUTER_API_KEY
;;        :ollama     none — needs `ollama` CLI on PATH and the model pulled
;;   3. Run: clj -M:run graphs/spec-generator.edn --project-dir ./spec-out
;;   4. Output: spec-out/output/<slug>.edn — review, then run it.

{:description
 "REPLACE THIS with a one-line description for SIMPLE/MEDIUM, or a full
  paragraph (multiple sentences, target stack, scope hints) for COMPLEX.
  Optionally end with a provider keyword (:ollama :kimi :glm :openrouter
  :anthropic). The generator strips the keyword from the slug/objective
  but uses it for model selection."

 :objective
 "You are a spec writer for a Clojure-based agent build system called Wiggum.
  Read the description and produce a single output EDN file that Wiggum can
  run directly to build the described system.

  Description:
  {{description}}

  === Phase 1: Analyse the description ===

  Write your analysis to STDOUT before touching any file.  Cover:

  1. Core deliverable — what gets built, what it does
  2. Inferred stack — language, frameworks, persistence; justify each choice
  3. Complexity classification — pick exactly one:
       SIMPLE   single script or utility, 1-2 files, no server, no persistence
       MEDIUM   full-stack app or service, 2-4 phases, standard persistence
       COMPLEX  multi-service system, 5+ phases, distributed concerns, architecture decisions
  4. Provider — scan the description for a trailing provider keyword:
       :ollama      local inference via Ollama (`ollama launch claude --model ...`)
       :kimi        Moonshot via Anthropic-compatible endpoint
                    (api.moonshot.ai/anthropic). Requires MOONSHOT_API_KEY.
       :glm         Z.ai (BigModel) Anthropic-compatible endpoint
                    (api.z.ai/api/anthropic). Requires ZAI_API_KEY.
       :openrouter  OpenRouter Anthropic-compatible bridge. Requires
                    OPENROUTER_API_KEY.
       :anthropic   Anthropic Claude API (default if no keyword present)
     Strip the keyword from the description before using it as the
     objective title or slug, but record it for model selection.
     If the chosen provider needs an env var, mention it in the analysis
     output so the user knows to export it before running the generated spec.
  5. Judge mode for the output spec — pick exactly one:
       :code      Software build. The deliverable is source code, tests,
                  configuration, deploy artifacts. The downstream judge
                  scores file-scope discipline, slop, hallucinated APIs.
                  Default for almost everything: web apps, services, CLIs,
                  libraries, data pipelines, dashboards, TUIs, MCP servers.
       :research  Fetch/read/synthesise content. The deliverable is
                  written analysis with citations: market briefings,
                  literature reviews, due-diligence reports, competitive
                  scans, summarisation runs over corpora. The downstream
                  judge scores citation discipline, faithfulness to
                  source, source diversity, recency, coverage.
     Pick by what the downstream run actually produces. A 'data analysis
     dashboard' is :code (the deliverable is the dashboard). A 'research
     report on the EV battery supply chain' is :research (the deliverable
     is the writeup).

     Also pick a :review-threshold that fits the work:
       :none    skip the judge entirely (no LLM call, no pauses). Use
                only when the user has explicitly asked for no review.
       :low     pause on any violation
       :medium  pause on medium or high
       :high    pause on high only
     Defaults: :high for :code, :medium for :research. Pick :none only
     when the user explicitly wants the run to grind through without
     stopping for quality review.

  6. Proposed phases and order.  Each phase header must open with a
     `Deliverables:` line listing the concrete files (or, for a phase that
     fleshes out an existing file, the routes/functions added).  The
     downstream supervisor lifts these directly into per-iteration
     `expected_files` — vague phase headers degrade to vague narrowing.
  7. Proposed :success-check command — single shell chain, file existence
     plus the project's native test runner.  No live smoke-tests, no
     setsid, no fuser, no docker build (see :success-check section in
     Phase 3).  Pick the test invocation idiomatic to the chosen stack;
     do not prescribe one — you already know how the language tests.
  8. Ambiguities and how you resolved them

  === Phase 2: Choose models ===

  Based on complexity classification and provider, select models for all four
  model fields in the output spec.  Use this table:

  ┌────────────┬──────────┬─────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┐
  │ Provider   │ Complex  │ :executor-model-config      │ :supervisor-model-config     │
  ├────────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ :anthropic │ SIMPLE   │ claude-haiku-4-5-20251001   │ claude-sonnet-4-6            │
  │ :anthropic │ MEDIUM   │ claude-sonnet-4-6           │ claude-sonnet-4-6            │
  │ :anthropic │ COMPLEX  │ claude-sonnet-4-6           │ claude-opus-4-7              │
  ├────────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ :ollama    │ SIMPLE   │ qwen2.5-coder:7b            │ qwen2.5-coder:14b            │
  │ :ollama    │ MEDIUM   │ qwen2.5-coder:14b           │ qwen2.5-coder:32b            │
  │ :ollama    │ COMPLEX  │ qwen2.5-coder:32b           │ qwen2.5-coder:32b            │
  ├────────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ :kimi      │ ANY      │ kimi-k2.5                   │ kimi-k2.5                    │
  │ :glm       │ ANY      │ glm-4.6                     │ glm-4.6                      │
  │ :openrouter│ ANY      │ anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5 │ anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5  │
  └────────────┴──────────┴─────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘

  The Anthropic-compatible providers (:kimi :glm :openrouter) don't
  publish a meaningful size ladder we can map to complexity — pick the
  flagship model the provider exposes via its Anthropic-compat layer.

  Also set :fallback-model:
    :anthropic  → 'claude-haiku-4-5-20251001'
    :ollama     → 'qwen2.5-coder:7b'
    :kimi       → 'kimi-k2.5'
    :glm        → 'glm-4.6'
    :openrouter → 'anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5'

  For non-Anthropic providers, add `:provider <kw>` to BOTH model config
  maps. For :glm / :openrouter / :custom also add the
  `:base-url` and `:auth-token-env` keys the morpheus
  `claude_code.clj/build-cmd+env` expects:
    :kimi        :base-url 'https://api.moonshot.ai/anthropic'
                 :auth-token-env 'MOONSHOT_API_KEY'
    :glm         :base-url 'https://api.z.ai/api/anthropic'
                 :auth-token-env 'ZAI_API_KEY'
    :openrouter  :base-url 'https://openrouter.ai/api/v1'
                 :auth-token-env 'OPENROUTER_API_KEY'
  :anthropic and :ollama need no extra keys.

  Do NOT emit `:temperature` or `:max-tokens` keys — morpheus does not
  thread them to the claude CLI. They're dead config and any post-edit
  validation will flag them.

  Also set :max-iterations from complexity:
    SIMPLE  → 8
    MEDIUM  → 20
    COMPLEX → 35

  For COMPLEX, also set :checkpoint-every to ceil(max-iterations / 5)
  so the human gets a periodic review pause on long runs. Omit the key
  for SIMPLE/MEDIUM.

  === Phase 3: Write the spec file ===

  Create output/ if absent.  Write output/<slug>.edn where <slug> is a
  kebab-case identifier derived from the description (provider keyword excluded).

  --- Depth scaling (CRITICAL) ---

  Scale the spec's depth to match complexity:

    SIMPLE  ~50-150 lines. One-screen objective with a few bullet points
            per phase. No :acceptance-criteria. The Kanban example below
            is the model.

    MEDIUM  ~150-500 lines. Each phase enumerates concrete files,
            functions, routes, and tests. :acceptance-criteria required
            with one shell-runnable check per phase.

    COMPLEX ~1000-4000 lines. Each phase enumerates every namespace,
            every public function, every database column, every tool
            schema, every test fixture. Constraints and anti-goals are
            domain-specific and exhaustive (15-25 of each is normal,
            not too many). :acceptance-criteria is mandatory and must
            include real shell commands gating phase advancement against
            real data — not just `clojure -M:test`. The reference for
            COMPLEX is `graphs/options-trader/options-trader.edn` in
            this repo: read it before writing a COMPLEX spec. Match its
            level of detail; do not produce a thin sketch and call it
            done.

  Under-detailing a COMPLEX spec is a defect. The supervisor will run
  fewer iterations against a thin spec because each iteration's
  `expected_files` will be vague, and the judge can't flag scope
  violations against expected_files that don't exist.

  --- Output schema ---

  The file must be a valid EDN map with exactly these keys in this order:

    :objective          — what to build, phase by phase.
                          Use === Phase N: name === headings.

                          Each phase header MUST be followed by a
                          `Deliverables:` block listing concrete files
                          (one per line) or, for phases that flesh out
                          an existing file, the specific routes/functions
                          added.  The supervisor lifts this list straight
                          into per-iteration `expected_files`, so vague
                          headers (no Deliverables: block, or prose-only
                          deliverables like 'a working server') produce
                          vague narrowing and lots of wasted iterations.

                          Be specific: file names, API routes, data shapes,
                          test expectations.

                          Context-window budget — HARD CAPS:
                            Whole :objective body must stay under 12000
                              characters (roughly 3000 tokens).  The
                              executor's iteration-1 CLAUDE.md embeds the
                              FULL :objective verbatim, and the supervisor
                              re-reads the original objective on every
                              iteration to plan narrowing.  An overlong
                              objective hijacks the context budget the
                              executor needs for the actual file bodies it
                              writes, and bloats the supervisor's prompt
                              every turn.
                            Per-phase body must stay under ~2000 chars.
                              If a phase needs more detail than that fits,
                              you have two options:
                                (a) describe the structure in the phase
                                    body (file list, function signatures,
                                    schema names) and let the executor
                                    fill in the bodies — Claude knows the
                                    idioms of every common stack, so do
                                    not re-teach them.
                                (b) lift the detail into a Phase-1
                                    deliverable file (`docs/phase-N.md`,
                                    `mockup.html`, `schema.sql`) that
                                    later phases reference.  Visual
                                    phases (mockup.html / mockup.txt /
                                    expected-session.txt) are the
                                    canonical example.
                            Never inline a verbatim file body longer than
                              ~50 lines.  Lift it to a Phase-1 deliverable
                              and reference it.  This is the most common
                              cause of bloat in generated specs.
                            Phase 4 validation rejects any spec whose
                              :objective exceeds the cap.

                          UI handling — pick the visual artifact based on
                          the kind of UI:
                            web/HTML        → output/mockup.html
                            terminal/TUI    → output/mockup.txt (ASCII
                                              layout boxes, like the panes
                                              shown in options-trader.edn
                                              Phase 7)
                            CLI w/ output   → output/expected-session.txt
                                              (sample command + expected
                                              stdout transcript)
                            none (library,
                            pipeline)       → skip the visual phase entirely

                          When a visual phase is needed, insert it AFTER
                          the skeleton phase and BEFORE any phase that
                          implements the UI:

                            === Phase N: Visual reference ===
                            Produce <output/mockup.html | mockup.txt |
                            expected-session.txt>. The artifact must
                            specify the full layout, components, and
                            interaction states. Subsequent phases must
                            match it exactly — no design values
                            (CSS, ASCII layout, transcript text) restated
                            elsewhere.

                          End with a Documentation phase covering README.md.

                          IMPORTANT — EDN string foot-guns inside this
                          string body:
                            * Embedded `\"` terminates the outer string.
                              For Clojure maps, JSON examples, Maven
                              coordinates: use single-quoted 'literals'
                              or backtick `code` style instead.
                              The options-trader.edn :tui block is the
                              model.
                            * Bare `\\` followed by a newline is NOT a
                              valid EDN escape — the strict reader fails
                              with `Unsupported escape character: \\`.
                              When showing a multi-line shell command
                              with line continuations, write `\\\\` (two
                              backslashes in the EDN source) so the parsed
                              string contains a single literal `\\`.
                            * Validate with `clojure.edn/read-string`
                              (strict) — `clojure.core/read-string` is
                              lenient and silently accepts both bugs.

    :project-dir        — nil

    :acceptance-criteria — string. REQUIRED for MEDIUM/COMPLEX, omit for
                          SIMPLE. Per-phase shell commands the supervisor
                          runs at the end of each iteration. Format:
                            --- Phase N (name) ---
                            Command:
                              <one shell command>
                            Required:
                              * <assertion the command exit/output proves>
                              * ...
                          The commands here MUST be more specific than
                          :success-check — :success-check verifies the
                          whole build at the end; :acceptance-criteria
                          gates one phase at a time.

    :success-check      — shell one-liner that exits 0 only when the
                          per-iteration deliverables are present AND the
                          project's tests pass.  Run by morpheus via
                          `sh -c <whole-string>`.
                          Hard rules:
                            Single command chain only — every line ends
                              with ` \\` continuation or ` && ` connector.
                              Trailing prose / Note: blocks / blank lines
                              followed by comment paragraphs are FORBIDDEN —
                              `sh -c` parses them as separate commands and
                              the exit code becomes the last prose line
                              (typically 127), so verification can never
                              report success.
                            Tests are MANDATORY, not optional — the chain
                              must invoke the project's native test runner
                              at the end of the chain (after file-existence
                              checks).  Pick the idiomatic command for the
                              chosen stack; this generator does not
                              prescribe a language.  File-existence checks
                              alone are NOT sufficient: the executor can
                              create a file with broken syntax that still
                              passes `test -f`, so without a test
                              invocation the verification is a smoke alarm
                              with the battery removed.
                            Per-iteration scope only — file existence
                              + tests.  Do NOT include `setsid`, `fuser`,
                              `nohup`, `kill $SERVER_PID`, port probes,
                              `curl` to localhost, `docker build`, or any
                              command that starts a long-lived process.
                              These are fragile across runs (port busy,
                              race with `sleep`, subprocess scoping under
                              `sh -c`) AND `setsid` / `fuser` do not exist
                              on macOS.
                            Live server smoke-test, `docker build`, and
                              `docker run` belong in a manual Phase-N step
                              documented in the README — never in
                              :success-check.
                            Tests must not call real external services
                              (network, real cloud APIs, prod databases).
                          If a visual phase is present, include
                          `test -f output/<artifact>` in the chain.

    :constraints        — vector of strings.  Always include:
                            'Work phase by phase — do not start phase N+1
                             until phase N tests pass'
                            'Run the test suite at the end of every iteration'
                          If a visual phase is involved, always add:
                            'Produce output/<artifact> in the visual phase
                             before writing any code in dependent phases'
                            'All phases after the visual phase must reference
                             output/<artifact> — do not restate design values
                             (CSS, ASCII layout, transcript) in the objective'
                          Add further stack-specific constraints.

    :anti-goals         — vector of strings.  Always include:
                            'Do not add dependencies not listed in the objective'
                            'Do not leave TODO comments or unimplemented stubs'
                          If a visual phase is involved, always add:
                            'Do not describe UI design in prose in the
                             objective — the visual artifact is the specification'
                          Add domain-specific anti-goals.

    :max-iterations     — integer from Phase 2 model selection

    :checkpoint-every   — integer for COMPLEX runs only (max-iterations / 5
                          rounded up). Omit for SIMPLE/MEDIUM.

    :step-once?         — false

    :generate-claude-md? — false

    :judge-mode         — keyword from Phase 1 step 5: :code or :research.
                          Wires the downstream judge to the matching rubric.
                          Required on every output spec; do not omit.

    :review-threshold   — keyword from Phase 1 step 5. Single switch that
                          controls the judge:
                            :none    skip the judge entirely (no LLM call,
                                     no pause). Use when the run is short
                                     enough that judging adds no value, or
                                     when the user explicitly does not want
                                     reviews.
                            :low     pause on any violation
                            :medium  pause on medium or high
                            :high    pause on high only (default for :code)
                          Default to :high for :code, :medium for :research.
                          Required on every output spec — never omit.

                          Note: the older `:review?` boolean flag is no
                          longer required. `:review-threshold :none` is the
                          single way to disable the judge.

    :fallback-model     — string from Phase 2 model selection

    :fallback-delay-ms  — 20000

    :executor-model-config  — map from Phase 2; include :provider /
                              :base-url / :auth-token-env keys for
                              non-Anthropic providers per Phase 2's
                              dispatch table. Do NOT emit :temperature
                              or :max-tokens — they are dead keys.

    :supervisor-model-config — same shape as executor.

  === Phase 4: Validate ===

  Use the exact <slug> the spec was written under. Do NOT use
  `ls output/*.edn | head -1` — that picks a stale file when prior
  generator runs have left specs behind.

  1. cat output/<slug>.edn to verify it looks correct
  2. clojure -e '(clojure.edn/read-string (slurp \"output/<slug>.edn\"))'
     — must exit 0. Use clojure.edn/read-string (strict) not bare
     read-string (lenient); strict catches embedded `\"` and other
     escape bugs the lenient reader silently allows.
  3. Required-key checks — all must pass:
       grep -q '^ :objective'           output/<slug>.edn
       grep -q '^ :success-check'       output/<slug>.edn
       grep -q '^ :constraints'         output/<slug>.edn
       grep -q '^ :anti-goals'          output/<slug>.edn
       grep -q '^ :max-iterations'      output/<slug>.edn
       grep -q '^ :generate-claude-md?' output/<slug>.edn
       grep -q '^ :executor-model'      output/<slug>.edn
       grep -q '^ :supervisor-model'    output/<slug>.edn
       grep -q '^ :judge-mode'          output/<slug>.edn
       grep -q '^ :review-threshold'    output/<slug>.edn
       grep -qE ':judge-mode +:(code|research)' output/<slug>.edn        ; valid value
       grep -qE ':review-threshold +:(none|low|medium|high)' output/<slug>.edn
     For MEDIUM/COMPLEX add:
       grep -q '^ :acceptance-criteria' output/<slug>.edn
     For COMPLEX add:
       grep -q '^ :checkpoint-every'    output/<slug>.edn
  4. If description implies a UI, exactly one visual artifact must be
     declared:
       grep -qE 'output/(mockup\\.html|mockup\\.txt|expected-session\\.txt)' output/<slug>.edn
       grep -q 'Visual reference' output/<slug>.edn
  5. Anti-pattern checks — all must be absent:
       ! grep -q 'kill $SERVER_PID'            output/<slug>.edn
       ! grep -qE ':temperature|:max-tokens'   output/<slug>.edn  ; dead keys
       ! grep -qE 'color: *#|border-radius: *[0-9]|font-size: *[0-9]' output/<slug>.edn
                                  ; tightened: only flag actual CSS values,
                                  ; not the substring 'color' in prose like
                                  ; 'colour palette' or 'colors:'.
       ! grep -q '<<<' output/<slug>.edn   ; placeholder text not stripped
       ! grep -qE 'setsid|fuser ' output/<slug>.edn
                                  ; Linux-only, fails on Darwin
       ! awk '/^ :success-check/,/^[^ ]/' output/<slug>.edn | grep -qE '^[[:space:]]*(Note:|#|;)'
                                  ; trailing prose / Note: / comment lines
                                  ; inside :success-check break sh -c
       ! awk '/^ :success-check/,/^[^ ]/' output/<slug>.edn | grep -qE 'setsid|fuser|nohup|docker (build|run)'
                                  ; live smoke-test commands forbidden in
                                  ; per-iteration :success-check
  6. Context-window budget check — must pass:
       OBJ_LEN=$(clojure -e '(count (:objective (clojure.edn/read-string (slurp \"output/<slug>.edn\"))))')
       test $OBJ_LEN -le 12000   ; under 12K chars total
     If this fails, the spec must be rewritten to lift detail into
     deliverable files; do not just trim prose to squeeze under the cap
     while leaving the structure unchanged.

  7. Tests-are-required check — :success-check must include a test
     invocation, not just file existence:
       awk '/^ :success-check/,/^[^ ]/' output/<slug>.edn \\
         | grep -qvE '^[[:space:]]*\"[[:space:]]*$' \\
         | grep -qE '(test|spec|check)[ -]|pytest|jest|vitest|mocha|cargo test|go test|rspec'
     If the project's test idiom is unusual, ensure the chain at minimum
     ends with a non-`test -f` command that exits 0 only when code is
     actually exercised.  A spec whose :success-check is purely
     `test -f` checks must be rejected.

  8. Print summary line:
       Spec written: output/<slug>.edn
       Provider: <provider>  Complexity: <complexity>
       Judge mode: <code|research>  Review threshold: <high|medium|low>
       Executor: <executor-model-id>  Supervisor: <supervisor-model-id>
       Phases: <N>  Visual phase: <kind|none>  Max-iterations: <N>
       Acceptance criteria: yes/no  Checkpoint-every: <N|n/a>

  === Reference spec — SIMPLE/MEDIUM (study the style — match it exactly) ===

  This is the model for SIMPLE/MEDIUM specs. For COMPLEX, also study
  graphs/options-trader/options-trader.edn in this repo — that file is
  the model for the depth and breadth a COMPLEX spec must reach.

  {:objective
   'Build a full-stack Kanban board application.

    === Phase 1: Skeleton ===
    Deliverables:
      package.json
      vite.config.ts
      server/index.ts
      db/schema.sql
      src/App.tsx (placeholder)
    - Vite + React + TypeScript project scaffold
    - Express + TypeScript server on port 3001
    - SQLite via better-sqlite3 at db/kanban.db
    - Single passing test in each of Vitest and Jest

